virtiofs: sync queues served by a hypercall on the calling vCPU

A request a task waits for goes on its CPU's sync queue, and an HVC (VIRTIO_FS_HVC_SYNC, a
vendor hypervisor call) has lodka serve that queue on the vCPU's own thread before it returns;
the request ends right there. No device thread to wake, no interrupt, no kworker, no wakeup of
the waiting task: a LOOKUP or GETATTR costs one exit to the host. Fsyncs, lock waits and I/O
over 128 KiB stay on the request queues, served by threads, as they would hold the vCPU; so
does a request the device answers VIRTIO_FS_SYNC_AGAIN, one on a file it would have to
download first.
Prior art: libkrun's VIRTIO_FS_F_SYNC, with an MMIO notify in place of the HVC.
